{
  "id": "h",
  "candidate": "h-r1",
  "heard": "Before comparing two models on perplexity, watch what happens to the exact same sentence under two tokenizers. The words stay put, only the cuts move. Hold the total surprise fixed and split the passage into more pieces. The per-token average falls, even though the score for the text as a whole hasn't changed. Count bits, divide by the raw UTF-8 bytes, and you have bits per byte. same text, same denominator. The models can still give it different probabilities. For a long time, language modeling benchmarks asked you to train and test on splits of the same corpus, like Penn Treebank or Wikitext 103. GPT-2 took a different route. Pre-train on broad web text, then measure zero-shot perplexity on those benchmark corpora without fitting a model to each one.",
